#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.15"
# ///
"""Evals: real Claude Code agents, run headless against throwaway fixtures, graded afterwards.

Each case under `evals/cases/<name>/` builds a fixture (a tasks repository with its board,
a code repository, one engineer's machine), gives an agent a prompt, performs the
scripted person steps between sessions, and grades the outcome with deterministic checks
outside the agent's reach. Every case ships a scripted reference run, which must pass
every check, and a null run, which must fail at least one; `validate` runs both, with no
model calls, and `./scripts/check` runs `validate`.

    eval validate [--case <name>]... [--plugin <git ref or folder>]
    eval run [--case <name>]... [--trials N] [--model M] [--arm with-plugin|without-skills]
             [--plugin <path or git ref>] [--container] [--image <tag>] [--results <dir>]
    eval gate [--base <ref>] [--all] [--trials N] [--model M]
    eval calibrate [--dimension <name>]... [--judge-model M]
    eval report [<results dir>] [--samples N]
    eval regrade <results dir>

`gate` is what the playbook's continuous-evals play does in CI, run by a person: when steering
paths (CLAUDE.md, skills, hooks, agents, commands) changed since the base it runs the regression
cases, or every case with `--all`, against the working copy, judges them, and writes a report
under `.eval-results/gate/` that `scripts/gate_evidence.py` verifies. It spends tokens.

The logic (cases, transcripts, board history, checks, statistics, cost) takes data and
returns data; git, files, subprocesses and the clock stay in the shell below it.
"""

